[EDITED AFTER FINISHING THE NG++, 100% Achievements]
This game has undeniable qualities:
1) Amazing map desing;
2) Aesthetically superb;
3) The multiplayer concept developed using phantoms of other players that can either help you or attack you is pure genius.
4) Unquestionable originality;
5) Incredible variety of weapons and weapon movesets;
6) ...
I used to plain hate this game, but then I "got gud" and I started to enjoy it, and understood why this game has such a huge fanbase: by forcing you to get good it becomes gradually more addictive. It feels good to become very proficient in something you used to suck at. But even this addiction is purely artificial: since you spent an incredible amount of time getting good, you feel forced to get back to the game simply because otherwise the fact that all those hours were a complete waste of time would be certified. But nevertheless, this game has one huge flaw which I can't ignore: it's difficult, but in all the wrong ways. In Dark Souls, getting good means:
1) Learning the geography of the maps and the location of traps/enemies;
2) Getting info about weapons moveset, scaling and upgrading (which in the game is completely neglected);
3) Learning the moveset of each enemy;
4) Remembering the location of shortcuts, items etc.;
5) Exploiting the flaws of the enemy AI;
6) Trying again and again and again...
In no other game community you see people talking almost exclusively about how to exploit the weaknesses of each boss movements and moveset, while the RPG elements are not only obscure thanks to the nonexistent in-game documentation, but also stripped to the bone and reduced to meeting basic weapon requirements and to a simple "which stat should I focus early on in the game", because in the long term you will upgrade almost all traits equally. Everything is calibrated to force you to put a lot of hours into farming, grinding and trial and error approaches to bosses and areas.
The clearest example of this artificial difficulty would be the two archers in Anor Londo: two archers with Dragonslayer Greatbows and infinite ammo start to snipe you from a distance where you can't even see them (let alone lock on them) while you have to fight two Bat Wing Demons in a pincher attack. From Software seems to want to tell you: "Don't worry, you can try again! We know you have plenty of time to waste." And yet, the fanboys and lots of reviewers have had the heart of saying that the game is difficult, but always fair. Yep. FAIR.
Of course, this is also why many love the Dark Souls saga: the reward of overcoming seemingly impossible tasks, and the reproducible emotions one feels when close to killing a boss or when struggling for survival in tight spots, knowing that a single error might be fatal and would destroy hours of effort. But in the end, all this amounts only to an enormous waste of time, especially when you realize that by learning you can get past all those challenges incredibly easily. I found myself taking out most of the bosses at the first try in the NG+, using the same gear as the previous run.
For instance, playing The Witcher 3 at Death March difficulty is always good challenge, but in this case one will focus on adequate battle preparation: studying the various enemies elemental/bomb/sign weaknesses, choosing the right armor set for one's play style etc., and not on learning how an AI behaves through trial and error or guide consultation.
Indeed, while in TW3, with a simple character customization mechanics one can approach in complete different ways to fight, in Dark Souls it all boils down to Melee player or Mage. At the same time, the game can be called difficult only when you're a complete stranger to it. I believe that anyone who re-played this game found himself clearing areas and bosses in 15 minutes or so.
If you seek for the transient reward of adrenaline and strong emotions, then Dark Souls will be the best possible choice in the entire Steam catalogue, but if you're looking for a truly meaningful experience you will find nothing here.
PS: of course, unless you want to brag about how good you are, in which personally I have no interest.

